+# Methodology
+
+What a good workout is, for the planner to plan by. A human writes this file (TRAINER.md, *Separation*); the engine never reads it and never enforces it (ADR-0001). Legality is the validator's; everything here is judgement.
+
+> **DRAFT.** Every number below is a proposal for the user to keep, change or strike. Sources were cited from memory and have not been checked against the papers.
+
+Scope: one person, general strength and muscle, body weight to dumbbells, workouts of 30-60 minutes. A goal chosen in onboarding (session 13) may override the defaults below.
+
+## 1. A working set
+
+| term | meaning |
+|---|---|
+| **working set** | one round of one exercise taken to **1-3 reps short of failure**. Warm-up sets are not working sets and are not prescribed. [4] |
+| **rep range** | **5-30** reps all build muscle when the set is hard [3]; **6-15** is the default, because it is close to failure without being a grind. |
+| **strength intent** | ≤ 6 reps, compound exercises only. |
+| **too easy** | an exercise the user can do for more than 30 reps is the wrong variant: choose a harder one (`compare_variants`, `regression_of`), never more reps. |
+| **timed work** | 20-60 s for planks and holds; a hold that lasts longer than 60 s is too easy, by the same rule. |
+
+## 2. How much
+
+| | default | source |
+|---|---|---|
+| working sets per exercise | **3-4** (2 for a third exercise on the same muscle) | [1] |
+| working sets per muscle, per workout | **6-10**; above 10 the extra sets buy little and cost the next workout | [1] |
+| working sets per muscle, per cycle | **10-20** | [1] |
+| exercises per group in a workout | **1** if the workout trains 3+ groups; **2** if it trains 1-2; **3** only for a group the workout is built around | |
+| exercises per workout | **4-7** | |
+
+A secondary muscle counts half: push-ups are chest sets and half triceps sets.
+
+## 3. Choosing the exercises
+
+1. **One movement before its variant.** Cover each pattern the groups need before a second exercise of the same pattern. Two variants of one exercise (`variant_of`) in one workout count as one exercise prescribed twice: choose one and give it the sets.
+2. **Compound before isolation**, the hardest and most technical first, core last.
+3. **A second exercise for a muscle must change something**: the angle, the stretch, or the pattern (a press and a fly, not two presses).
+
+## 4. Rest, by intent
+
+| intent | rest after the round |
+|---|---|
+| strength (≤ 6 reps, compound) | **2.5-3 min** [7] |
+| hypertrophy, compound | **2 min** [2] |
+| hypertrophy, isolation | **60-90 s** |
+| core, timed work | **45-60 s** |
+| a superset of non-competing exercises | the longer of the two exercises' rests, minus the other exercise's work |
+
+Short rest is not a way to fit more into the time. Shorter rests cost reps on the next set [2, 5].
+
+## 5. The structure of a block
+
+**Straight sets are the default.** A superset or a circuit has to have a reason that is not *it fits*.
+
+| structure | when | never |
+|---|---|---|
+| **straight sets** | the main compound of each pattern; anything heavy, technical or with setup | |
+| **superset** | two exercises sharing **no** primary muscle: antagonists (push and pull), upper and lower, or a lift and core. Saves time without costing the work [5, 6]. | two exercises for the same primary muscle |
+| **circuit** (3-4 exercises) | a **reduced workout** (the user has less time than the plan needs), or a conditioning finisher at the end | more than 4 exercises; two exercises of one pattern or one primary muscle; the main lift of the day |
+
+A block that would hold two exercises for the same muscle is two blocks of straight sets.
+
+## 6. Filling the time
+
+The time is filled with **sets, not exercises**. Under the time, in this order:
+
+1. add a round to each block, up to the per-muscle ceiling in §2;
+2. lengthen rests to the top of their band in §4;
+3. add an exercise for a pattern or muscle the workout has not yet trained.
+
+Over the time, the reverse: drop the last isolation exercise, then a round, then pair non-competing straight sets into supersets.
+
+## 7. A mesocycle
+
+| | default |
+|---|---|
+| cycles a mesocycle repeats for | **4-6** |
+| same exercises throughout | yes (ADR-0008); only the loads, reps or variant move |
+| progression | until the scheme exists (ROADMAP, *After 1.0.0*): the user adds load or reps when the top of the range is reached on every set |
+
+## Example
+
+Chest and arms, 45 minutes, body only and bands. The plan the bot made: two circuits, nine exercises, four push-up variants, 25 minutes. The same request by this document, costed before setup:
+
+| block | exercises | rounds × reps | rest | min |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| straight | `Pushups_With_Feet_Elevated` | 5 × 8-12 | 150 s | 15.0 |
+| superset | `Cross_Over_-_With_Bands` + `Band_Skull_Crusher` | 4 × 12 | 90 s | 10.8 |
+| straight | `Bench_Dips` | 4 × 10-15 | 90 s | 8.4 |
+
+Chest 9 sets, triceps 8 plus 2.5 from the push-ups, 34 minutes. It hits the ceiling in §2 before it reaches issue 03's 40-minute floor, and the catalog has **no biceps exercise** for body only or bands. See open question 1.
+
+## Open questions for the user
+
+1. **Ceiling against floor.** When a workout reaches its per-muscle ceiling under the floor of ticket 11.3, what gives: a group nobody asked for (core is the obvious one), longer rests, the ceiling, or the floor?
+2. **Intent.** Until onboarding picks a goal, is the default hypertrophy (6-15 reps) or strength (≤ 6 on the main lifts)?
+3. **Proximity to failure.** Nothing in the plan's shape carries RIR. Is 1-3 RIR said in the block's `reason`, or does it wait for a field?
+4. **Circuits.** Do you ever want one outside a reduced workout?
+5. **A muscle the equipment cannot reach** (biceps above): leave it untrained and say so, or work it as a secondary?
+
+## Sources
+
+1. Schoenfeld, Ogborn, Krieger (2017). Dose-response relationship between weekly resistance training volume and increases in muscle mass: a systematic review and meta-analysis. *J Sports Sci* 35(11).
+2. Schoenfeld et al. (2016). Longer interset rest periods enhance muscle strength and hypertrophy in resistance-trained men. *J Strength Cond Res* 30(7).
+3. Schoenfeld, Grgic, Ogborn, Krieger (2017). Strength and hypertrophy adaptations between low- vs. high-load resistance training: a systematic review and meta-analysis. *J Strength Cond Res* 31(12).
+4. Refalo et al. (2023). Influence of resistance training proximity-to-failure on skeletal muscle hypertrophy: a systematic review with meta-analysis. *Sports Med* 53.
+5. Robbins, Young, Behm, Payne (2010). Agonist-antagonist paired set resistance training: a brief review. *J Strength Cond Res* 24(10).
+6. Weakley et al. (2017). The effects of traditional, superset, and tri-set resistance training structures on perceived intensity and physiological responses. *Eur J Appl Physiol* 117(9).
+7. American College of Sports Medicine (2009). Progression models in resistance training for healthy adults. *Med Sci Sports Exerc* 41(3).
